(docs/'backend/BE-003_009_LEASE_GUARD_TRANSACTION_OWNER.md').write_text('''# Preserve the outer runtime-lease submission lock

The existing PostgreSQL lease store rolled back its private connection after every read and every guard exit. A nested read, the runtime fence that calls that read, or an inner guard therefore released the outer `FOR UPDATE` row lock before its submission scope ended. An independent actual PostgreSQL actor could take over the expired timestamp while that scope was still active. Three baseline cases reproduced, with a positive control that observed the peer waiting on an actual database lock. This is source arbitration evidence, not a claim that a native trade occurred.

The private store now has one reentrant connection/transaction owner. Same-thread reads and inner guards borrow the outer transaction; other threads wait for that owner. Only the outer exit releases its locks. Reentrant mutation and close refuse before touching authority. Body failures retain their original exception. No lease key, logical alias, public field, owner grant, CAS or epoch rule is replaced.

An intermediate repair passed670 source cases but then failed two further actual SQL-error probes: PostgreSQL aborted the parent transaction even without an explicit rollback. Borrowed reads and inner-guard queries now use native savepoints, preserving the parent row locks after a recoverable server-side error. The stable final run passes672 actual PostgreSQL/SQLite/HTTP/runtime/accounting/authority cases, zero skips or storage-audit violations, all1162 pinned source/test hashes matching and the owned fixture independently absent.67focused checks pass. Twelve new cases cover reads, fences, nesting, Python and actual SQL errors, body errors, attempted writes/closes and cross-thread reads; peer waits and later epoch takeover are read from the real database. Counts overlap. Database connection loss, host/provider partitions and the full native programme still require independent qualification.

The first test attempt failed four cases before the functional boundary because its isolated database had no lease table. Initializing the actual PostgreSQL ledger owner corrected that fixture; the original assertions were retained. Both that failed attempt and the intermediate two SQL-error failures remain in raw evidence. Earlier670does not qualify the stable later source. Canonical mutation preflight remains374cases/383edits with no findings; that is not execution.

Physical-account duplicate-alias arbitration remains unimplemented. Preserve logical aliases and signed owner controls; do not substitute a native hash for the existing alias namespace or reject valid observer profiles. Shared outgoing collateral/order reservations, all-origin account-wide risk, native provider/cohort and all remaining backend/Android work remain required. See `../receipts/LEASE_GUARD_SOURCE_20261027.json` and raw `../ci/lease_guard_20261027/`. Zero whole-unit, native, production, economic or S24 acceptances. No frontend redesign precedes BG-001.
''')
(docs/'backend/BE-009_PHYSICAL_ACCOUNT_ARBITRATION_INSPECTION.md').write_text('''# Physical account arbitration continuation

Inspection only; no physical-account lease or native exposure qualification is issued.

The active account registry permits multiple aliases for one native account. Existing runtime leases and owner/recovery state use the logical alias. The earlier persisted-registry/InMemoryLeaseStore probe was primitive-only; no end-to-end native financial bypass is established. The actual cTrader factory uses `int(account.login)` and chooses its official transport host from server/demo metadata. Native account identity, provider/realm/host, logical alias and verified observed account binding are distinct concepts.

Preserve logical alias arbitration as well as any additional physical-account arbitration. Simply replacing alias keys can allow two different physical accounts with the same alias to collide in alias-scoped financial/recovery owners, and can invalidate signed controls. Multiple observer/profile aliases remain valid. Inspect all sending runtime constructors and final submission guards, canonical broker identities, epoch/state bindings, retirement/recovery and the admitted cohort before implementation. MT5 and MT5_EA may address the same underlying account; Deriv client app identifiers do not identify separate customer accounts. Do not infer identity from currency, a display name or an arbitrary digest.

The PostgreSQL connection-owner repair in BE-003/009 is a prerequisite for composing guards: a nested read/guard previously released its parent lock, and real SQL errors required native savepoints. Complete its committed qualification first. Any later physical scope must preserve alias fencing, current owner authority, safe expiry/takeover/release, account-wide financial lineage and explicit old-cohort migration/unknown-effect holds. Provider proof and independent/native admission remain separate. No new owner authorization, grant format, frozen-package amendment, registry rejection or frontend design is created by this note.
''')
